From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] ui/cocoa: the Mac's input methods type into the VM (mac-ime, experimental)

What: with PINGOUIN_IME_SOCKET set (OmacVM.app sets it only for a VM with the
feature mac-ime on, together with the virtio port app.pingouin.ime), QEMU's
view becomes an NSTextInputClient. The VM's Fcitx5 module (src/ime/guest)
says over the port which kind of field has the focus and where its caret
is. While a text field has it, the Mac's input source is an input method
and the VM has the keyboard, keys without Cmd or Control go to the view's
NSTextInputContext (also keys from the full-grab tap): marked text goes to
the guest as preedit (code points, clauses), chosen text as commit; keys the
input method does not want (Return, arrows, Backspace with nothing marked,
its Latin mode's own letters) go to the VM as keys, QEMU's way; the release
of a key it used is not sent. The candidate window is placed at the guest's
caret, mapped through the display layout the display port already keeps
(output, black rows, the window of that output); with only the window known
(Chromium, foot) at the last click after the focus, else the window's
bottom left. In a text field, macOS's input source shortcuts (hot keys 60
and 61, while macOS's own shortcuts are off for the VM) and a globe key set
to "Change Input Source" switch the Mac's input source instead of going to
the VM. Leaving the field, the window or the input method drops what was
marked. The Mac never reads the guest's text.
When only the guest's field goes (the VM keeps the keyboard), the input
method's context goes off 0.2 s later unless a field comes back: a
terminal takes its focus again right after Enter, and some input methods
(WeChat Input) go back to their first mode at each off and on (#357).

Why: #273 (requested and scoped by @Vocllum): Chinese, Japanese and Korean
with the Mac's own input methods and candidate window in Omarchy
(docs/adr/0043-mac-ime.md).

Off: without PINGOUIN_IME_SOCKET pingouin_ime_on stays false; handleEvent:
checks it before anything else and the globe key's tap only after its own
test, so every key takes QEMU's path byte for byte as before, and the view
has no input context (as NSView without the protocol).

Security: the guest is untrusted. Lines over 4 KiB are dropped, numbers
checked (ui/pingouin-ime.h), types checked before use (NSJSONSerialization
values); the guest can only move the candidate window or keep keys going
to the VM. Nothing new is allowed: no entitlement, no permission.

Upstream: not applicable (OmacVM's guest side).
Test: app/runtime/Tests/keys/test-ime.sh (the rules, and this patch's hooks
in the patched ui/cocoa.m); typing on a test VM (docs/adr/0043-mac-ime.md).

